#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/id #uuid "a3f9e797-4bfb-5b2a-b460-98d7def3ae20" :question/math-academy-id "e-6754" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/id #uuid "83fc553b-3ad0-5003-afe3-81204a695162" :question/math-academy-id "e-6755" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "a2641aac-7c3a-5a2c-97f0-aca4782c7215" :question/math-academy-id "e-7220" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cf8c59-80ca-5c6c-b50e-e796548538f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "0c25fa5d-ccc5-55e4-b3e1-06492f830655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295363} {:answer/id #uuid "074bbb58-87f2-52d5-992f-e8e7e621ed0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295364} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd5ab0e-5ef6-5a77-9dbe-a0573330be0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365} {:answer/id #uuid "07372dce-4325-5ece-bc95-8233bc42e4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34cf8c59-80ca-5c6c-b50e-e796548538f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d8dedf-331e-5615-8a14-a67f86043945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1796fbf1-69e9-550e-8c0e-6de576d0b219" :question/math-academy-id "q-117408" :question/problem "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "751c92b4-3b48-546b-902e-6679a757225c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571} {:answer/id #uuid "e80728a9-3025-5675-8bbd-4f6c11fc4c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278572} {:answer/id #uuid "485d1a06-bc45-55d7-bc84-3ff478f6c358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573} {:answer/id #uuid "41e81461-54b6-55ff-bb29-74298379cdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278574} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2cea99-320f-5686-80a6-703358b8b96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "751c92b4-3b48-546b-902e-6679a757225c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571} :answer-field/id #uuid "1375ee2f-e69d-5865-9f2a-987fca463960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dbc0e3f-52e0-5c91-bb08-00ee163a5979" :question/math-academy-id "q-117412" :question/problem "$10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df6bac1f-d8e9-5116-875b-af3f5937d3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269006} {:answer/id #uuid "a869a4b0-def1-5612-ba46-74eca03db1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269007} {:answer/id #uuid "5c38c4ee-59e2-5a7c-8290-aa4dc9046baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269008} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4426bc-0611-5b36-b910-1f1a929aef44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269009} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6e582e-88a6-5b88-a68a-2a60ae829c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d6e582e-88a6-5b88-a68a-2a60ae829c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269010} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dded7bc-0e76-5704-885a-26fdcb09c959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e64fd775-535a-57c6-92d2-90544b5aea0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117462" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1de734-7e89-5775-be96-708770a71621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} {:answer/id #uuid "ae92e186-89d2-5d1b-80fb-ad5312c685a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "d892dc8f-e331-55db-8bee-de86e7e620b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295385} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3a434a-a6c5-5eb7-ae9c-86d22b2f38c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295386} {:answer/id #uuid "1421e17c-d81d-5463-8845-a52eade9f26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1421e17c-d81d-5463-8845-a52eade9f26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5132a217-dfb9-5445-a0bd-3fe3bc49b29f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8183a61b-58d8-541a-bebb-58f44b6bf474" :question/math-academy-id "q-24756" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={30}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c44efe-fe76-53f6-a455-a545204a8a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057995} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcc216d-542e-5d4d-a3a5-97fc859039ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057996} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bbfb43-4144-52a3-96f9-08c29a1d72e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057997} {:answer/id #uuid "f24fbacc-ec85-5fbc-93d8-cba3fac2844f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057998} {:answer/id #uuid "45768302-197e-5850-972e-da1b6e477983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45768302-197e-5850-972e-da1b6e477983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057999} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f979b5-c765-50db-be2f-3dd4dce45129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057994}] :question/id #uuid "0d5bf39c-c2e8-5873-b9ee-51b70f57dcba" :question/math-academy-id "q-24758" :question/problem "What is $\\arctan (\\sqrt{3})$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259570} :answer-field/id #uuid "1afda0f2-c499-5fb4-a591-efc3a186f139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1e3d3bc-82fc-5fa2-90ce-eb77683f685b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320665" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf82005b-33ef-575c-949a-89c143c51948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf82005b-33ef-575c-949a-89c143c51948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269023} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f53033-bd9c-54d0-a344-513494b55726" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ce7784f-f6e3-515b-8004-feee143d1026" :question/math-academy-id "q-320721" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f3cb27-22ab-5228-96a1-c2f3b98c731f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269026} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c7a204-1c72-5500-b0aa-a41bfc3f0c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f3cb27-22ab-5228-96a1-c2f3b98c731f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269026} :answer-field/id #uuid "06cea244-ce37-5115-afe3-ae4fc4bb0319" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7893b97-a032-5b93-8a30-5368e64fd2a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-320893" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
